+## Record product conversations
+
+The Runtime service automatically enables trajectories for persisted,
+non-debug conversations when these environment variables are configured:
+
+```dotenv
+NEXENT_EVENT_TRAJECTORY_ROOT=/mnt/nexent/events
+NEXENT_EVENT_TRAJECTORY_COMPRESSION=none
+```
+
+The root must be absolute and persistent. Session ids are opaque SHA-256-based
+identities scoped by tenant, user, and conversation; browser callers never
+provide a Session id or filesystem path. Fork siblings share a stable logical
+working directory so a child Session can be resumed without changing the DSH
+`cwd` contract.
+
+The Runtime image installs the SDK's `event` extra. If the root is empty,
+debug runs are used, or a run has no persisted `conversation_id`, Event
+recording remains disabled.
+
+## Record an SDK-only run
 
 Pass an explicit `EventTrajectoryConfig` when constructing `AgentRunInfo`:
 
@@ -83,6 +103,24 @@ fork accepts only a stable boundary outside an open turn. The resulting JSONL
 artifacts are the common Runfold Event format and can be restored directly by the
 TypeScript SDK and rendered by the Trajectory UI.
 
+Nexent's authenticated product endpoints expose the same operations without
+accepting arbitrary Session ids:
+
+```text
+GET  /conversation/{conversation_id}/trajectory?from_seq=0&limit=1000
+POST /conversation/{conversation_id}/trajectory/fork
+```
+
+The fork request accepts an optional stable Event `boundary` and child `title`.
+It first validates the Event prefix, then copies exactly the corresponding
+completed Nexent turns—including visible units, citations, images, attachment
+references, agent selection, chat mode, knowledge scope, and runtime metadata—
+into the child conversation. History-summary checkpoints are intentionally not
+copied because they contain database ids from the parent; the child's context
+manager can create a fresh checkpoint after it continues. A normal subsequent
+`/agent/run` on either conversation is the Event resume path; the existing SSE
+`resume=true` parameter remains only a transport reconnection mechanism.
+
